# Foundation: AME Tool Operating Principles

## Purpose
Enforce a consistent, production-grade workflow across all AME Tool work. All agents must:
- Practice TDD: write tests first, implement minimal code to pass, then refactor.
- Avoid regressions: preserve existing behavior unless explicitly changed with tests.
- Keep code clean and maintainable (SOLID, separation of concerns, layering).
- Automate validation: run full test suite and build before proposing a PR.

## Global Directives
- Prefer clear, copy-pasteable outputs (code, tests, config, instructions).
- Always propose tests first (unit, integration, e2e where applicable).
- Include edge cases and failure paths; add regression tests for any bug fixed.
- Continually improve docs and developer experience; provide runnable examples.
- If ambiguity exists, assume safest implementation + add TODO with rationale.
- Never promise future background work—perform tasks fully in this session.

## Definitions of Done (DoD)
1. Tests are written and passing locally and in CI.  
2. Build succeeds (no type/lint errors, no failing static analysis).  
3. Backward compatibility preserved unless ADR approves a breaking change.  
4. Documentation and changelog updated.  
5. PR description includes: intent, approach, tests, risks, roll-back.  

## Guardrails
- Don’t delete tests to make CI pass; update or supersede with justification.  
- Don’t introduce non-deterministic tests or hidden network calls.  
- Prefer small, focused PRs.  